# Print arguments that fail, at today's baseline. Two behaviours are pinned
# here because Step 6 of the PIR plan deliberately changes both:
#
#   - an out-of-bounds argument materializes its zero and still prints, so the
#     failed slot shows as "0" rather than disappearing;
#   - a failed value-position conditional suppresses the *whole line*, taking
#     its successful siblings with it.
#
# Under per-slot printing a failed argument omits only itself, siblings still
# print, and separators appear only between emitted slots. The multi-argument
# cases are what distinguish that from whole-line suppression; the owned heap
# temporary and the ranged access are what keep cleanup honest on both paths.
# Still to add when Step 6 lands: a false heap-valued conditional beside
# successful siblings, so the skipped outcome's own temporary is leak-checked.

arr = [10 20 30]
v1 = 5
v2 = 6

"--oob first--"
arr[9], v1, v2

"--oob middle--"
v1, arr[9], v2

"--oob last--"
v1, v2, arr[9]

# An inline concatenation is an owned temporary, unlike a named binding, so a
# skipped sibling must not leak or double-free it.
"--oob beside owned heap temp--"
"s" ⊕ "t", arr[9], v1

# A ranged checked access: in-bounds iterations print values, out-of-bounds
# iterations print the materialized zero today and will omit the slot later.
"--ranged checked--"
j = 0:5
v1, arr[j]

"--cond middle--"
k = 0:5
v1, (k > 3) + 0, v2

"--end--"
